WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.660
Data Protection isolates applications and purposes so protected payloads are not broadly interchangeable.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:16.760
That isolation is useful because most applications should not be able to read each other's cookies, tokens, or internal protected values just because they run on the same platform.

3
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.800
Some scenarios intentionally share protected payload behavior.

4
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:28.180
A common example is sharing authentication cookies across related ASP.NET Core applications.

5
00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:40.840
That can support a unified user experience, but it requires careful alignment of key storage, application name, cookie name, authentication scheme, domain, path, and deployment environment.

6
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.480
Sharing should be deliberate, documented, and limited to applications that truly need it.

7
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:56.500
Multi-app scenarios create risk if one application has weaker access controls, weaker hosting, broader operator access, different release practices, or looser logging.

8
00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:01.800
A shared cookie can connect the reliability and security posture of multiple apps.

9
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:09.280
Deployment slots and staging systems should not accidentally share production protected payload behavior unless specifically designed.

10
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:16.420
A staging app that can read production cookies or protected state may create confusing user behavior and governance concerns.

11
00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.520
Environment boundaries should be reviewed as carefully as app boundaries.

12
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.800
The practical question is whether sharing is necessary and whether every participant meets the same expectations.

13
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.460
If the answer is yes, align the configuration intentionally.

14
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:38.200
If the answer is no, keep application names, key rings, cookie settings, and environments separated.
